This builds on `checkMemberOnServer` from before. It treats a local "no" as "not sure yet" rather than a final answer, and only the server can give a final no.

```ts
type LocalMember = { id: string; name: string; expiresAt: string; active: boolean };

type AccessDecision =
  | { allowed: true;  source: "local" | "server" }
  | { allowed: false; reason: "denied" | "unverifiable" | "invalid_id" };

const localMembers = new Map<string, LocalMember>(); // filled by the nightly sync

// Scans of the same ID that are already waiting on the server share one request
const inFlight = new Map<string, Promise<MemberCheck>>();

function isValidLocally(m: LocalMember | undefined): boolean {
  return !!m && m.active && new Date(m.expiresAt).getTime() > Date.now();
}

function lookupOnServer(memberId: string): Promise<MemberCheck> {
  const existing = inFlight.get(memberId);
  if (existing) return existing;

  const p = checkMemberOnServer(memberId).finally(() => inFlight.delete(memberId));
  inFlight.set(memberId, p);
  return p;
}

async function decideAccess(rawId: string): Promise<AccessDecision> {
  const memberId = rawId.trim();
  if (!/^[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,64}$/.test(memberId)) {   // adjust to your real ID format
    return { allowed: false, reason: "invalid_id" };
  }

  // 1. Fast path: the local list says they're good
  if (isValidLocally(localMembers.get(memberId))) {
    return { allowed: true, source: "local" };
  }

  // 2. Not in the list, or listed but inactive/expired. The list may be up to
  //    24h stale, so ask the server before saying no.
  const result = await lookupOnServer(memberId);

  switch (result.status) {
    case "active":
      localMembers.set(memberId, { ...result.member, active: true });
      return { allowed: true, source: "server" };
    case "expired":
    case "not_found":
      localMembers.delete(memberId); // drop any stale local entry
      return { allowed: false, reason: "denied" };
    case "unavailable":
      return { allowed: false, reason: "unverifiable" };
  }
}

async function handleScan(rawId: string) {
  const decision = await decideAccess(rawId);

  if (decision.allowed) {
    openDoor();
  } else if (decision.reason === "unverifiable") {
    showDenied("Can't verify right now, please see the front desk");
  } else {
    showDenied("Membership not found or expired");
  }

  logScan(rawId, decision); // include decision.source / reason so you can measure the fix
}
```

Notes on the choices:

- **Expired locally also falls through to the server.** Depending on how your renewal works, a renewed member might be missing from the list, or present with their old expiry date. This handles both.
- **Failed lookups are never cached.** A member who renews a minute after being denied gets in on the next scan.
- **Successful lookups are cached.** Repeat scans, such as a member re-entering after a locker-room trip, stay instant and don't depend on the network.
- **In-flight deduplication.** People often scan twice when the door doesn't respond immediately, and this stops that from sending two requests.
- **ID validation.** Garbage scans never reach the server.
- **Logging the source.** After rollout, you should see the "not found" denials drop to near zero, and a count of `source: "server"` entries tells you how many scans the fallback rescued. If `unverifiable` shows up often, the tablets' network connection is the next thing to look at.

Two things to decide:

1. **When the server is unreachable**, this denies entry. Some gyms prefer to let people in if they were valid in the last list, or if they have a recently expired membership (a grace window), and sort it out later. That's a policy call for you, and it would be a small change in the `unavailable` case.
2. **Nightly sync behavior.** If the sync replaces the whole map, server-cached entries disappear, which is fine because the new list will include them. But make sure the sync doesn't wipe the map before the new data has finished downloading, or scans during that window will all go to the server.

